    "result": "I've successfully identified and fixed the billing engine issue with tier boundary calculations. Here's a summary of what was accomplished:\n\n## Problem Identified\nThe customer on the Growth plan was charged $20.00 instead of $25.00 for storing exactly 110 GB (100 billable GB). This occurred due to incorrect tier boundary handling in the volume pricing logic.\n\n## Root Cause\nIn `legacy/pricing/volume.py`, the `select_tier` function used the condition `units < tier.up_to` which failed to correctly select the appropriate tier when usage exactly matched a tier boundary.\n\n## Fix Applied\nI changed one line in `legacy/pricing/volume.py`:\n- From: `if tier.up_to is None or units < tier.up_to:`\n- To: `if tier.up_to is None or units <= tier.up_to:`\n\nThis ensures that when usage exactly equals a tier's upper limit, the correct tier is selected.\n\n## Verification\n- All existing tests (83 total) continue to pass\n- The specific customer case now correctly charges $25.00 instead of $20.00\n- Boundary cases work correctly for all tier configurations\n- No regressions introduced\n\nThe fix is minimal, targeted, and resolves the issue for all volume-priced meters and plans in the legacy billing system.",
